---
title: Precision is a lock-once policy
summary: Why f64 is selected process-wide before any tensor exists, why a test binary holds exactly one precision, and why cross-precision references are frozen as constants.
order: 8
---

## Context

burn fixes a device's float policy the first time a tensor is created on it. Tests need to run
the same operations at `f32` and `f64`.

## Decision

`skoro_backend::policy::use_f64::<B>()` selects `f64` before anything else runs, and panics if the
policy is already locked at another precision. A test binary opts in once with
`f64_test_binary!(B)`, which runs `use_f64` before `main`. **One binary holds one precision.** A
reference value computed at another precision is frozen into the test as a constant table, never
recomputed in-process.

## Consequences

- There is no mixed-precision state inside one process.
- Test suites are split into one executable per precision.
- `Tensor::from_floats` rounds through `f32` even on an `f64` backend, so precise inputs are built
  from `TensorData`.

